 1. Improve pacing and balance. The first enemy wave launches at 2.5 seconds, before the player can
     meaningfully prepare. In idle simulations, every seed produced defeat around 176 seconds. Add an
     opening grace period, then scale wave size and production over time.

  2. Add a minimap. The 120×120 battlefield is much larger than the viewport, while unexplored space is
     nearly black. A minimap showing explored terrain, friendly units, the camera rectangle, and visible
     threats would solve the largest usability problem.

  3. Make battles easier to read. Add persistent health bars for damaged units, move/attack destination
     markers, selection-type details, clearer projectile impacts, and warning cues when the keep is
     attacked.

  4. Expand strategic variety. The simulator produced identical results for seeds 1, 7, and 23.
     Difficulty profiles or AI personalities could vary attack timing, preferred composition, aggression,
     and defensive reserve size.

  5. Revisit unit composition. The idle AI produced 17 swordsmen, 2 shields, and no new archers. That
     suggests the production scoring strongly converges on swordsmen. Run broader balance simulations and
     expose summary metrics such as damage by unit type, survival time, and essence efficiency.

  6. Add explicit attack-move and stop commands. Right-click currently combines movement and attacking.
     Separate move, attack-move, focus-attack, and stop/hold behavior would make player control more
     predictable.

  7. Improve onboarding. The menu’s large panel is mostly empty. Use it for compact unit roles, core
     controls, difficulty selection, or a short tutorial scenario. Tooltips on recruitment cards could
     explain health, damage, speed, and tactical role.

  8. Add progression and objectives. The current match is purely “destroy the other keep.” Resource
     nodes, control points, unit upgrades, defensive structures, or secondary objectives would create
     decisions beyond accumulating units.

  9. Strengthen responsive layout. The window is resizable, but HUD cards use fixed pixel coordinates. A
     narrow window will eventually overlap or clip controls. Calculate card widths and positions from the
     current window size.

  10. Split the single 1,900-line module. Separate simulation/model, enemy AI, rendering, interface, and
     input handling. This would make new modes and content much safer to implement without disturbing
     combat logic.
